Experimental SIMD and parallel KLU refactor
=========================================

The optional real refactor path reuses KLU's initial factorization, pivots,
block structure and U traversal. Within each L column, entries are sorted by
row for vector access; dependency levels allow independent columns to run on
OpenMP workers. Small levels are combined into serial stages. Initial
factorization, complex factorization and triangular solves are not parallelized
by this change. Circuit equations and simulation tolerances are unchanged.

Build with the bundled KLU, for example in a directory outside the source:

  /path/to/source/configure --with-x=no --with-readline=no \
      --enable-klu --without-suitesparse --enable-openmp
  make -j8

Keep .option klu in the deck; before analyses in .control, set:

  set klu_parallel
  set num_threads=8

The feature is off by default. A system SuiteSparse build always follows its
existing refactor path, including when klu_parallel is set: its private storage
is deliberately not interpreted by this prototype. Non-OpenMP bundled builds
can use the serial optimized path. x86-64/GCC dispatches AVX-512/AVX2/scalar at
runtime; other supported builds use scalar updates. Separate multiply/subtract
instructions avoid introducing FMA in the evaluated GCC build. Do not use
fast-math when checking arithmetic reproducibility.

Normal refactor/solve accumulation order is retained under the tested compiler
settings. Sorting L may change roundoff in transpose solves, so byte-identical
transpose/adjoint results are not promised. Use fresh processes for baseline
comparisons: unsetting the flag cannot undo in-memory L sorting.

The plan is owned by the ngspice matrix wrapper, freed before its numeric
object, and rebuilt when numeric/symbolic objects or requested threads change.
Scratch is private per worker and capped at 512 MiB. Allocation failure or
active nested OpenMP execution uses the original refactor. Zero/nonfinite block
pivots trigger full recomputation through the original routine, preserving its
singular status and halt policy without adding Gmin twice. A late allocation
failure may leave sorted L columns for the original routine.

Levels need >=4 columns and estimated work >=32768 for parallel execution.
Matrices need estimated work >=100000 and a non-singleton block to use the
fast path. These empirical thresholds do not guarantee a speedup; planning
and synchronization can still cost more than they save. The summary counts
workers that actually performed factor arithmetic, not just team size.

Tests
-----

After an OpenMP bundled build, using a new output directory:

  python3 tests/klu/parallel/run-klu-parallel.py \
      --build /path/to/build --output /path/to/results

The direct harness compares independent original and optimized numeric objects.
48 configurations cover nonsymmetric/permuted BTF matrices, requested
1/8/16/32 threads, scaling -1/0/1/2, changed coefficients and singular/recovery
paths. Normal solutions must be byte-identical and have componentwise backward
error <=1e-10. Transpose residuals are tested on well-scaled fixtures. The
generated circuit suite compares 24 configurations, including real/complex/real
analysis transitions and a matrix large enough to activate the plan. Its saved
arrays and accepted/rejected timepoint counts must match. No PDK is needed.

The high-dynamic-range fixtures also serve as diagnostics, not unconditional
passing references: on the ngspice-47 prototype, disabling scaling produced
~1.93e-7 normal backward error in both implementations despite identical
answers. Scaled transpose gave ~2.06e-10 original / ~2.37e-10 optimized, both
above the 1e-10 gate. The positive suite does not relax the gate to turn those
results into passes; it uses well-scaled cases for those modes.

klu_alloc_fault.c is linked only into an allocation-failure test with
--wrap=malloc,--wrap=calloc and NG_KLU_ALLOC_TEST. NG_KLU_TEST_MAX_SIMD is a
test-only compile override for scalar/AVX2 coverage. Neither is enabled in a
normal executable. Sanitizer coverage refers to the changed KLU library and
direct harness, not an entirely instrumented simulator.

Performance context
-------------------

Before this pre-master-48 port, the same arithmetic/scheduling implementation
was measured on a maintained ngspice-47 branch, GCC 11.4, Xeon 6982P-C/AVX-512.
Three alternating-order complete-transient pairs per circuit used the same
executable, eight requested threads and scientific settings; only this feature
flag changed. Median paired analysis speedups were 1.114x for a 2304-node 2D
RC network, 1.753x for a 4096-node 3D RC network and 1.418x for a SKY130 PEX
opamp (915 MOS, 56344 R, 13618 C). A 4096-MOS model-dominated circuit was
essentially unchanged. The opamp total-process speedup was 1.235x because
~32 seconds of netlist loading was unaffected. All 12 pairs had identical
saved arrays and timepoint counts. These are prototype-branch measurements,
not a claim that this upstream port was measured at those exact speeds.

The 2D case used one matrix worker, so its gain is from SIMD, not multicore
scaling. The 3D and PEX cases used eight. This does not establish universal
speedup or performance relative to Spectre. No proprietary solver source was
used; KLU derivation and licensing remain covered by COPYING and the bundled
SuiteSparse license files.
